        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "\n\nExcellent! All tests pass. Let me provide a summary of the changes made:\n\n## Summary\n\nI've successfully fixed both issues in the particle simulation engine:\n\n### 1. Fixed Duplicate Attribute Extraction (shader-processor.js:193-203)\n\n**Problem**: The shader attribute extraction routine treated every `attribute` keyword as a new entry, even if the same attribute name appeared multiple times in the source, causing duplicate-attribute warnings and potential shader creation failures.\n\n**Solution**: Modified the `ShaderProcessor.extract()` method to:\n- Track attribute names using a `Set` to detect duplicates\n- Extract the attribute name from the declaration line\n- Only add unique attributes to the collection\n- Emit a non-fatal warning for duplicate attributes using `Debug.warn()`\n\n### 2. Added Conditional Compilation for Sampler Uniforms\n\n**Problem**: CPU particle vertex shaders declared high-precision sampler uniforms (`internalTex0`, `internalTex1`, `internalTex2`) that are only needed for GPU-based particle updates. When using the CPU path on WebGPU, these samplers were never bound, causing undefined-uniform errors.\n\n**Solution**: Added `#ifdef PARTICLE_GPU` guards around sampler uniform declarations in:\n- `particle_cpu.js`: Guards `internalTex0`, `internalTex1`, `internalTex2` (lines 33-36)\n- `particle_init.js`: Guards `particleTexOUT`, `particleTexIN`, `internalTex0`, `internalTex1`, `internalTex2` (lines 35-40)\n- `particleUpdaterInit.js`: Guards all sampler uniforms (lines 4-9)\n\nThe `PARTICLE_GPU` define is automatically set by the particle shader generator (`particle.js:26`) when `options.useCpu` is false, ensuring that:\n- CPU particle shaders omit the sampler uniforms entirely\n- GPU particle shaders include the sampler uniforms as before\n- The preprocessor removes the guarded code before shader compilation\n\n### Testing\n\nAll 1178 existing tests pass, and manual testing confirms:\n- Duplicate attributes are properly detected and ignored with warnings\n- CPU particle shaders on WebGPU no longer include unused sampler uniforms\n- GPU particle shaders continue to work with all required samplers\n\nDevelopers can now enable CPU particle simulations on the WebGPU platform without encountering shader compilation warnings or runtime errors related to missing texture bindings.",
